column6:
Yes – approval required to operate payment systems or offer payment services: 
-account issuing services; 
-electronic money issuing services;
-merchant acquiring services;
-other payment system operations or payment-related products

column8:
No explicit separate authorisation for cross-border payments.

PSPs conducting cross-border activities are subject to:
- Stricter cooperation and contractual requirements with overseas counterparties;
- AML/CFT compliance obligations for cross-border transactions;
- Requirement to process domestic payment transactions within Indonesian territory;
- Prohibition on using crypto assets as a payment source of funds.

Foreign principals already operating as PIP prior to PBI 10/2025 may benefit from grandfathering provisions, subject to submission of a letter of guarantee to BI.

Under the regulatory framework established by Bank Indonesia, there is no separate cross-border payment license. Instead, cross-border payment capabilities are treated as an extension of an entity's licenses. 
To conduct cross-border transactions or utilize payment channels for cross-border purposes, an authorized PJP or PIP must granted taking into account:
the principle of reciprocity; (ii) equivalence of risk management standards; (iii) the benefits to the Indonesian economy; and (iv) compliance with statutory provisions.

column9:
BI processes applications through three stages:

1. Administrative review — completeness and accuracy of submitted documents;

2. Substantive analysis — across four aspects:
   a. Institutional: legality, ownership structure, control, Board of Directors and Board of Commissioners composition, and fit and proper test (Uji Kemampuan dan Kepatutan) for all key persons;
   b. Financial and capital: minimum paid-up capital (initial capital), ongoing capital adequacy, feasibility analysis, and business projection;
   c. Risk management: legal, operational, liquidity, and technology risks; and
   d. TIKMI capability (Transaksi/Transaction, Interkoneksi/Interconnection, Kompetensi/Competence, Manajemen Risiko/Risk Management, Infrastruktur TI/IT Infrastructure) — the new five-factor assessment framework introduced by PBI 10/2025 that determines PSP classification and eligible activity bundle;

3. On-site visit (or request for additional operational-readiness documents in lieu thereof).

Non-bank entities (LSBU) applying for Bundle 1 must be incorporated as a limited liability company (perseroan terbatas); Bundle 2 and Bundle 3 applicants may be other Indonesian legal entities.

PSP Classification: Based on TIKMI results and criteria of size, interconnectedness, complexity, and substitutability, BI classifies each PSP as Primary PSP (PSP Utama) or Non-Primary PSP (PSP Selain PSP Utama). This classification determines the activity bundle eligible, ongoing capital level, and supervisory intensity.

Business Planning: PSPs must submit a Strategic Business Plan (SBP, 3–5 years) and a Payment System Business Plan (RBSP, short-term), approved by BI, as a condition of and during the licence/designation.

Others as deemed necessary by BI.
